course: dictionary
lesson: 12202
title: Confounded: puzzled, annoyed or proved wrong
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word confounded can mean three different things, so the clues around it matter. «The twins were confounded by the magic trick, and could not work out how it was done.» The clue is could not work out, so confounded means very puzzled.
explain: «This confounded door will not open!» The clue is the angry exclamation, so confounded means annoying. It is an old-fashioned way of showing you are cross, like saying «this annoying door».
explain: «The rain confounded our plans for a picnic.» Plans are what we expect to happen, so confounded here means the rain went against our plans and proved them wrong. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then test your meaning in the sentence.
fact: Confounded is the past form of the verb confound.
fact: Confounded can mean very puzzled or confused, as in «confounded by the puzzle».
fact: As mild, old-fashioned swearing, confounded means annoying, as in «that confounded policeman!» from The Wind in the Willows.
fact: To confound a plan or prediction is to prove it wrong.
fact: «Confound it!» was an old way of saying «Bother!».
exercise: «The twins were confounded by the magic trick.» What does confounded mean here? | Very happy and proud | *Very puzzled and confused | Very quick at running | Very wet from the rain
exercise: «This confounded door will not open!» What does confounded show? | *Annoyance | Happiness | Fear | Hunger
exercise: «The result confounded the scientists, who had expected the opposite.» What does confounded mean here? | Made the lab very cold | Made them laugh | *Proved their expectation wrong | Gave them a prize
test: «The detective was confounded by the clues, which made no sense at all.» What does confounded mean? | *Puzzled and confused | Pleased with the clues | Bored and sleepy | Angry at the clues
test: Ellie says, «Confound it!» What does this old phrase mean? | Hooray! | Be quiet | Thank you | *Bother!
exam: Which sentence uses confounded to mean annoying? | The children were confounded by the puzzle. | The storm confounded the sailors' plans. | *«That confounded bike has a flat tyre again.»
